  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above average temperatures through late week, with a \nwarm front bringing the chance for some scattered light showers \nlater tonight into Thursday, before a cold front generates a round \nof beneficial rain showers Thursday night into the day on Friday.\n\nAny lingering fog/low clouds will mix out this morning, with dry and \nseasonably warm weather today.\n\nA warm front will lift across western NY late tonight into Thursday \nmorning, then into the North Country Thursday afternoon. Enhanced \nforcing on the nose of a 30-40 knot LLJ combined with increasing \nmoisture advection will bring growing chances for some scattered \nshowers during the second half of tonight into Thursday morning from \nthe Finger Lakes westward, shifting northeast into the North Country \nby the midday/afternoon hours. Strong WAA pattern will limit mixing \npotential of the stronger winds off the deck, however at least \npartial mixing along with a tightening pressure gradient ahead of an \napproaching cold front will elevate southwesterly flow. This will be \nespecially true northeast of the Lakes, where winds could gust 25-30 \nmph, possibly 35 mph northeast of Lake Erie. \n\nA sharper trough will then dig across the upper Great Lakes forcing \nthe aforementioned cold front southeast through western and \nnorthcentral NY late Thursday night into Friday morning. While PWAT \nvalues will be nearing the 100th percentile for this time of year, \nalong with a stout 35-45 knot LLJ, showers will be mainly anafrontal \nand thus not be able to take advantage of the more plentiful \nmoisture and much stronger forcing ahead of the front. While there \nwill be the likelihood for a period of beneficial showers, the heavy \nrainfall/flooding threat will be near zero. Although some timing \ndiscrepancies remain, the main window for more persistent showers \nwill be late Thursday night through midday/early afternoon Friday \njust behind the cold front, with dry weather and temperatures \nreturning to near normal readings for the first half of the weekend.",
